  "slug": "instructions-follow-full-collection",
  "title": "Instructions Follow — Full Collection",
  "summary": "Our daily environment has become a video game tutorial, filled with security tape, directional arrows, and protocols. Disembodied voices and smart objects constantly instruct us. The pandemic amplified this need for direction. As we adapt to changing circumstances, apps and platforms use algorithms to influence our behavior.\n\n\"Instructions Follow\" explores our relationship with digital devices and information systems, reflecting on how codes, regulations, and defaults govern our present.\n\nWorks by Lauren Lee McCarthy and Thierry Fournier confront viewers with digital clones of the artist's voice or automated systems questioning humanity, alluding to fears of machine replacement and adaptation to inhuman systems. Joana Moll and Guido Segni critique techno-solutionism and the labor conditions of cloudworkers.\n\nAlba G. Corral, Disnovation.org, and Martin John Callanan explore software-enabled creativity and algorithmic serendipity. Corral's generative composition considers emergence, Disnovation.org's bot creates artwork descriptions, and Callanan presents a 15-year log of Photoshop actions as a creative diary.\n\nInstructions and codes have always shaped societies. The gamification of habits is another example of following rules. Alix Desaubliaux and Antoine Schmitt use game language to highlight these rules, connecting the \"Magic Circle\" to physical spaces or creating an interactive maze. Desaubliaux also links programming code to shaping reality, akin to incantations. Solimán López, Irma Marco, and Varvara & Mar connect digital technologies with ancient rituals, transforming social media content into altar pieces.",
